---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import ContactBand from '../components/ContactBand.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="A software builder who leads. Alexander Thomas on engineering leadership, hands-on architecture, and putting AI to useful work."
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="A little about me"
    title="A builder who leads."
    description="I like turning unclear, consequential problems into software people can use and teams can confidently own."
  />
  <section class="shell page-body about-grid">
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>
        I’m Alexander Thomas. Alex is fine. I’m Chief Technology Officer at
        Applied Visions, where I work with organizations and engineering teams
        to build new products, modernize existing systems, and put AI to
        practical use.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’ve spent nearly two decades moving between business strategy and
        technical execution. The work has ranged from consumer mobile
        applications and transit systems to multi-tenant SaaS platforms and
        enterprise AI.
      </p>
      <p>
        The common thread is getting close enough to the problem to make good
        decisions, then staying involved long enough to see those decisions
        through.
      </p>
      <h2>Leadership with technical context.</h2>
      <p>
        My leadership work spans more than 40 people across engineering, QA,
        product, and design. I care about giving teams clear priorities, useful
        feedback, and enough context to make decisions without waiting for
        permission at every step.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also stay hands-on. Architecture, code, delivery pipelines, and
        production behavior keep strategy honest. Understanding the details
        helps me ask better questions and recognize when a team needs a
        decision, a different approach, or simply room to work.
      </p>
      <h2>AI, with an operating plan.</h2>
      <p>
        I build AI-powered products and help teams adopt AI-assisted
        development. That includes implementation, review, testing, and the less
        glamorous work of making a change sustainable across real projects.
      </p>
      <p>
        I start with the work, not the model. Some problems need interpretation
        and judgment. Others need dependable rules. Either way, ownership,
        permissions, evaluation, observability, and cost belong in the design.
      </p>
      <h2>A few chapters along the way.</h2>
      <div class="timeline">
        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span>2026–now</span>
          <div>
            <h3>CTO, Applied Visions</h3>
            <p>
              Technical direction, practical AI adoption, software
              modernization, and product delivery.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span>2019–2026</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Principal Solutions Architect, Applied Visions</h3>
            <p>
              Solution architecture and technical delivery across custom
              products, enterprise AI, and modernization programs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span>2019–now</span>
          <div>
            <h3>CTO & Product Owner, MealTrack</h3>
            <p>
              Connecting roadmap, architecture, customer commitments, and
              delivery for a multi-tenant SaaS platform.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span>2009–2019</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Engineering & technical leadership, Applied Visions</h3>
            <p>
              Desktop, mobile, web, cloud, and integration systems, including
              transit and consumer applications.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="timeline-item">
          <span>2007–2009</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Applications Developer, Computer Associates</h3>
            <p>
              Enterprise web applications, backend integrations, and data
              pipelines.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <aside class="profile-aside">
      <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
      <h2>Strategy to source code.</h2>
      <dl>
        <div>
          <dt>Currently</dt>
          <dd>
            CTO at Applied Visions
            <br />
            CTO & Product Owner at MealTrack
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Based in</dt>
          <dd>Apex, North Carolina</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Experience</dt>
          <dd>Nearly 20 years in software delivery</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Education</dt>
          <dd>
            BS, Computer Science
            <br />
            Stony Brook University
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>My technical home base</dt>
          <dd>
            .NET / C# · React · TypeScript
            <br />
            Azure · Distributed systems · Applied AI
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      {site.linkedin && (
        <a class="text-link" href={site.linkedin}>
          Connect on LinkedIn <Icon name="external" size={16} />
        </a>
      )}
      <br />
      <a class="text-link" href={site.github}>
        Find me on GitHub <Icon name="external" size={16} />
      </a>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section class="shell section border-top">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
        <h2>A few things I come back to.</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="principles">
      <article class="principle">
        <span>01 / CONTEXT</span>
        <h3>Start with the actual problem.</h3>
        <p>
          Understand how the business works before deciding what the technology
          should do. A useful question often beats a clever solution.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle">
        <span>02 / OWNERSHIP</span>
        <h3>Make responsibility clear.</h3>
        <p>
          Give people the context and authority to do good work. Build systems
          that someone can understand, maintain, and operate.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="principle">
        <span>03 / JUDGMENT</span>
        <h3>Keep the tradeoffs visible.</h3>
        <p>
          Cost, security, delivery time, and maintainability all count. The best
          choice depends on the problem and the team that will live with it.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <ContactBand title="Good work starts with a conversation." />
</Base>
